WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.910
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.877 --> 00:00:09.460
EMILY YE: Developers
looking for a way

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:11.140
to store secrets for
their applications

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:13.870
like credentials often turn to
the popular secret management

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:15.775
tool HashiCorp
Vault. In this video,

00:00:15.775 --> 00:00:18.150
we'll cover how to authenticate
the HashiCorp Vault using

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:19.570
Google Cloud Credentials.

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:21.670
I'm Emily, an engineer
working on Google Cloud

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:23.950
Platform and open source
integrations between Cloud

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:24.784
and HashiCorp Vault.

00:00:24.784 --> 00:00:27.200
ANDY MANOSKE: And I'm Andy, a
product manager at HashiCorp

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:28.024
and the Vault team.

00:00:28.024 --> 00:00:29.440
Vault is a secrets
management tool

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:32.380
that enables organizations
to essentially secure, store,

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:34.297
and access control
secrets across distributed

00:00:34.297 --> 00:00:35.630
infrastructure and applications.

00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:38.130
EMILY YE: In the last video,
we deployed our '80s video game

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:41.170
application Hyperspace on Google
Cloud Platform using Terraform.

00:00:41.170 --> 00:00:44.020
Today we'll be changing gears a
bit and talking about security.

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:46.570
Let's say we launched Hyperspace
a year ago to great success,

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:48.880
and now we're looking to
monetize our game by letting

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:50.440
players buy extra lives.

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:52.759
In order to do that, we
need to accept payments.

00:00:52.759 --> 00:00:54.550
However, the API key
to our payment gateway

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:55.524
is very sensitive.

00:00:55.524 --> 00:00:57.190
If we were to lose
it, someone malicious

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:59.130
could possibly steal our money.

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:01.120
This payments API key
is what many developers

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:02.390
refer to as a secret.

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:04.870
We hear this use case
a lot from customers.

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:06.370
How should I securely
store secrets

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:08.680
that my applications
need at build or runtime?

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:10.720
Developers working in
public cloud or multicloud

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:13.510
environments need a way to
store credentials, configuration

00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:15.970
files, and many other secrets
for both their applications

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:16.900
and themselves.

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:18.970
Andy's going to show us
how to solve this on GCP

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:21.660
using Vault, an open
source tool by HashiCorp.

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:22.660
ANDY MANOSKE: Yes, I am.

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:25.780
HashiCorp Vault manages storing,
encrypting, and managing access

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:26.730
to secrets.

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:28.590
It has multiple
authentication back ends,

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:31.131
which provide different options
for using your own identities

00:01:31.131 --> 00:01:32.920
to identify users
in Vault. Vault

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:34.720
also has many secret
back ends for managing

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:36.971
different kinds of secrets,
like the payments API key.

00:01:36.971 --> 00:01:39.345
EMILY YE: Can you explain what
an authentication back end

00:01:39.345 --> 00:01:39.850
is, Andy?

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:40.690
ANDY MANOSKE: Great question.

00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:42.640
So Vault behaves conceptually
similar to a website

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:44.240
with multiple
authentication methods.

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:46.323
Just like you can log in
with Google on a website,

00:01:46.323 --> 00:01:47.860
administrators can
configure Vault

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:51.250
to allow users or machines to
log in and access other secrets

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:53.020
in Vault. Vault
is also pluggable,

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:54.459
so you can use a
built-in back end

00:01:54.459 --> 00:01:56.750
or write your own authentication
method if you need to.

00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:57.708
EMILY YE: That's right.

00:01:57.708 --> 00:02:00.160
The LDAP or GitHub auth
methods are great options

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:02.620
for developers, since they
allow users to log in with LDAP

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:03.730
or a GitHub API key.

00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:06.840
In our case, our video game
is running on GCE instances,

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:09.100
so the GCP auth back
end is a great choice.

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:09.940
ANDY MANOSKE: That
does sound perfect.

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:10.880
How does it work?

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:11.860
EMILY YE: Well,
using this back end,

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:14.380
developers or applications can
authenticate to Vault using

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:16.285
two types of GCP credentials.

00:02:16.285 --> 00:02:19.450
The IAM option lets you use
your IAM service account,

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:22.130
and the Google Compute Engine,
or GCE, metadata option

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:25.090
allows your GCE instances
to use signed VM metadata

00:02:25.090 --> 00:02:26.059
tokens to authenticate.

00:02:26.059 --> 00:02:27.850
ANDY MANOSKE: So the
video game GCE service

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:29.710
can either use
their GCE metadata

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:31.510
or their associated
IAM service account

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:32.565
to authenticate to Vault?

00:02:32.565 --> 00:02:33.190
EMILY YE: Yeah.

00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:35.810
I think the GCE method
is especially cool.

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:38.320
Since a metadata token
can be obtained on startup

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:40.021
without additional
authorization,

00:02:40.021 --> 00:02:42.520
you will need to hardcode any
initial secrets into a startup

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:45.490
script or set super secret
environment variables.

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:46.980
Yay, no hardcoding!

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:48.490
ANDY MANOSKE: Should we show
how the authentication back end

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:48.990
works?

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:50.520
EMILY YE: Let's do it.

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:52.152
All right, so for
this demo, we've

00:02:52.152 --> 00:02:54.610
already got a Vault server up
and running on a separate GCE

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:55.985
instance, and
someone has already

00:02:55.985 --> 00:02:58.260
stored our payments API
key, as you can see.

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:00.520
In this terminal, we first
shelled into the instance

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:02.170
that our Vault
server is running on.

00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:04.294
ANDY MANOSKE: First, an
admin for your Vault server

00:03:04.294 --> 00:03:06.682
will need to enable the
authentication back end.

00:03:06.682 --> 00:03:08.140
EMILY YE: Next,
the back end treats

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:10.070
GCP as a trusted
third party, so it

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:12.400
needs credentials to
make calls to GCP APIs

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:14.070
to confirm identity.

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:16.360
The admin can set these
credentials using the config

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:18.616
endpoint.

00:03:18.616 --> 00:03:21.120
Next, the admin
needs to add roles.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:23.290
Vault has the concept of
policies, which determine

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:25.030
what secrets a user can access.

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:26.740
Roles bind these
predefined policies

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:28.990
to generated auth tokens
to restrict what secrets

00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:30.760
can be accessed by a user.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:33.430
On our server, we've already
added the payments policy that

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:35.480
allows access to our API key.

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:37.177
So let's add a GCE role.

00:03:37.177 --> 00:03:39.760
ANDY MANOSKE: We mentioned that
the back end allows either GCE

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:42.020
instances or IAM service
accounts to authenticate.

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:43.729
Is that what the GCE
type argument means?

00:03:43.729 --> 00:03:44.770
EMILY YE: That's correct.

00:03:44.770 --> 00:03:47.110
It means the back end will
expect GCE metadata when

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:48.840
authenticating under this role.

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:51.190
We've also added a
project ID and bound zone

00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:53.320
to only authorized
any GCE instances

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:54.570
in this project and zone.

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:57.150
We can also use things like
instance groups, labels,

00:03:57.150 --> 00:03:59.680
or associated service accounts
to limit what instances

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:01.340
can log in under this role.

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:03.340
ANDY MANOSKE: So now the
GCP back end is set up.

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:04.860
How do we actually
authenticate to Vault?

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:06.234
EMILY YE: Let's
switch terminals.

00:04:06.234 --> 00:04:09.130
We've shelled into one of our
game's front-end VMs here.

00:04:09.130 --> 00:04:12.010
The GCE authentication
method requires an instance

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:13.850
to present an identity
metadata token.

00:04:13.850 --> 00:04:16.100
So let's actually get this
from the metadata server.

00:04:19.967 --> 00:04:21.550
Note that this request
doesn't require

00:04:21.550 --> 00:04:22.810
any further authentication.

00:04:22.810 --> 00:04:26.040
The metadata is returned on
the JSON Web Token, or JWT,

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:27.294
signed by Google.

00:04:27.294 --> 00:04:29.710
This is what the raw metadata
contained in the token looks

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:30.890
like.

00:04:30.890 --> 00:04:33.220
Note it includes the VM's
identifying information,

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:34.600
as well as an expiration time.

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:36.970
This JWT expires
within an hour, so we

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:39.587
don't have to worry that it
will be stolen and reused.

00:04:39.587 --> 00:04:40.420
ANDY MANOSKE: Great.

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:41.990
Now that you have
this metadata JWT,

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:44.500
you can just pass into Vault and
request an authentication token

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:46.125
under the role that
we created earlier.

00:04:46.125 --> 00:04:48.027
EMILY YE: Let's do that.

00:04:48.027 --> 00:04:50.110
ANDY MANOSKE: Vault will
confirm the VM's identity

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:52.442
and return an auth token and
lease information showing

00:04:52.442 --> 00:04:53.650
how long this token is valid.

00:04:55.542 --> 00:04:57.250
EMILY YE: Now that we
have this token, we

00:04:57.250 --> 00:05:06.370
can use it to get our
payments API key from Vault.

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:08.550
And now we have our API key.

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:11.560
So with that, we have Vault up
and running on GCP with a GCP

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:13.690
back end for authentication,
and our developers

00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:15.877
can keep their
payments API key safe.

00:05:15.877 --> 00:05:18.460
Andy, if other developers have
questions when setting this up,

00:05:18.460 --> 00:05:20.280
where can they get
more information?

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:22.570
ANDY MANOSKE: So check out
vaultproject.io and look up

00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:24.645
documentation on the
GCP auth back end.

00:05:24.645 --> 00:05:26.770
It's been great to work
with Google Cloud on Vault.

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:27.853
EMILY YE: Thank you, Andy.

00:05:27.853 --> 00:05:28.357
I agree.

00:05:28.357 --> 00:05:30.940
You can also check out our blog
post linked below that details

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:33.250
all of your authentication
options and other HashiCorp

00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:34.920
and Google collaborations.

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:36.980
See the link in the
description below.

00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:38.932
EMILY YE AND ANDY MANOSKE: Bye.

00:05:38.932 --> 00:05:40.640
EMILY YE: Thanks,
everyone, for watching.

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:43.540
We hope you found this series on
using HashiCorp Vault with GCP

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:44.387
helpful.

00:05:44.387 --> 00:05:45.970
Check out the links
in the description

00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:49.290
below, and don't forget to
subscribe to the channel.

